<h1>Powered &amp; Live Roller Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A powered roller conveyor uses an external motor to drive cylindrical rollers, transporting flat-bottomed products at a controlled speed. The three conventional drive methods are belt-driven live roller (BDLR), line-shaft, and <a href="/conveyor-types/pallet-conveyors/">chain-driven live roller (CDLR)</a>. Powered roller conveyors move cases, cartons, totes, and pallets through automated <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a> and distribution systems.</p>
<h2 id="drives-heading">Three Drive Methods Compared</h2>
<h3>Belt-Driven Live Roller (BDLR)</h3>
<p>A BDLR conveyor uses a continuous belt running beneath the rollers. Pressure rollers hold the belt against the underside of the carrying rollers, and friction turns them. A single gearmotor drives the belt through a drive pulley, with a take-up to maintain tension.</p>
<p>Because drive force comes from belt contact, it can be adjusted. In Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2015_682_190acc.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">190-ACC manual</a>, suspension angles support the pressure rollers that hold the drive V-belt against the tread rollers, and knurled nuts set the pressure. The primary maintenance items are belt tension, tracking, and pressure adjustment, plus periodic belt replacement.</p>
<h3>Line-Shaft Roller Conveyor</h3>
<p>A line-shaft conveyor uses a continuously rotating steel shaft running the length of the conveyor beneath the rollers. Drive spools ride on the shaft, and a polyurethane O-ring band connects each spool to a roller. A single gearmotor drives the shaft. Hytrol's 190-NSP is one example of this design.</p>
<p>The O-ring drive acts as a slip clutch: if a product is held, the spool slips on the shaft rather than stalling the motor. This allows low-pressure accumulation without active zone control and makes line-shaft a good fit for pop-up transfers. The shaft must stay clean: Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2015_677_190nsp.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">190-NSP manual</a> lists oil on the line shaft as a cause of rollers not turning under load.</p>
<p>The major maintenance item is O-ring replacement. O-rings stretch and break over time, and each one can be replaced individually.</p>
<h3>Chain-Driven Live Roller (CDLR)</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> use roller chain and sprockets to drive rollers. Each roller carries sprockets, and chain loops link adjacent rollers. A gearmotor drives the chain, which positively drives every roller with no slip.</p>
<p>CDLR handles the heaviest loads of the three. Capacity is rated per linear foot: Hytrol's <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/category/3251/26-crr-heavy-duty-straight-chain-driven-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">26-CRR</a> is rated at 300 lbs per foot with supports on 10-foot centers or 1,000 lbs per foot with supports on 5-foot centers. The positive chain drive keeps rollers turning regardless of product weight, which is why CDLR is the usual choice for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyor</a> applications and heavy case transport.</p>
<h2 id="comparison-heading">BDLR vs Line-Shaft vs CDLR</h2>
