<td>Light to medium cases and totes</td>
<td>Pallets, drums, heavy unit loads</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Accumulation</td>
<td>Zero-pressure (pneumatic zones) or minimum-pressure (adjusted drive pressure)</td>
<td>Low-pressure by O-ring slip, zero-pressure with zone brakes</td>
<td>Zero-pressure with a gearmotor per zone</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Key Maintenance</td>
<td>Belt tension, tracking, pressure</td>
<td>O-ring replacement, clean shaft</td>
<td>Chain lubrication and tension</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Best For</td>
<td>Medium-duty cases</td>
<td>Light-medium cases, diverting</td>
<td>Heavy pallets, drums</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Main Cost Drivers</td>
<td>Gearmotor, belt, pneumatic zones if used</td>
<td>Gearmotor, shaft and spools, zone brakes if used</td>
<td>Heavy rollers and frame, chain, gearmotors</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Speed and load ratings vary by model, so compare data sheets for the specific models quoted. For built-in zone control without pneumatics, see <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a>. For <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">detailed pricing factors</a>, see the cost guide.</p>
<h2 id="zone-heading">Zone Control and Accumulation</h2>
<p>Standard powered roller conveyors run all rollers continuously. Adding zone control allows individual sections to start and stop independently. Zone control converts a transport conveyor into an <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a> that can buffer products without backpressure. <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/accumulation/zero-pressure-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's zero-pressure line</a> includes belt-driven, line-shaft, and chain-driven models, each built differently.</p>
<h3>BDLR Zone Methods</h3>
<p>In Hytrol's belt-driven zero-pressure model (ABEZ), each zone has a zone controller, a pressure frame that applies and removes drive, and a pneumatically operated brake. When a zone is occupied and the next zone downstream is full, the zone stops driving. The ABEZ manual sets the regulator to a working pressure of 35 psi. This approach needs compressed air and valves in each zone.</p>
<h3>Line-Shaft Zone Methods</h3>
<p>In Hytrol's line-shaft zero-pressure model (190-NSPEZ), air bags in each zone inflate to stop that zone from driving, working with brake pads. Its manual sets the regulator to 40 psi. Without zone control, O-ring slip still allows low-pressure accumulation.</p>
<h3>CDLR Zone Methods</h3>
<p>Chain-driven zero-pressure conveyors split the conveyor into zones, each with its own gearmotor. In Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2015_661_crezd.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CREZD models</a>, each zone has a zone controller, a zone starter box, and a gearmotor, so no compressed air is needed.</p>
<h3>MDR Alternative</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR (Motor Driven Roller) conveyors</a> provide zone control as a built-in feature without pneumatics or mechanical clutch hardware. Each zone has its own low-voltage DC motorized roller and photo-eye sensor. This makes MDR a common choice for new zero-pressure accumulation installations carrying lighter loads. MDR costs more per foot than a continuous-run BDLR or line-shaft transport conveyor but removes compressed air from the accumulation design.</p>
<h3>Accumulation Modes</h3>
<p>Powered roller conveyors support three accumulation modes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Zero-pressure accumulation (ZPA):</strong> Products stop in zones with gaps between them. No product-to-product contact. Protects fragile items from crushing and label scuffing.</li>
<li><strong>Minimum-pressure accumulation:</strong> Products queue with light contact. On belt-driven models such as Hytrol's 190-ACC, drive pressure is adjusted so it just moves the heaviest item, and the rollers slip under stopped product. Hytrol states <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/accumulation/minimum-pressure-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">minimum-pressure conveyors</a> can be adjusted to bring backpressure to 2% of the live load. Acceptable for durable products like corrugated cases.</li>
<li><strong>Slug accumulation:</strong> Products accumulate in groups (slugs) and release together. Used for batch release applications like <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a> and palletizer layer forming.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Powered Roller Applications</h2>
<p><strong>End-of-line packaging:</strong> Powered roller conveyors connect case packers, case sealers, labelers, checkweighers, and palletizers in a continuous automated line. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">Packaging line integrators</a> use live roller sections for case transport and accumulation zones before palletizers.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution and order fulfillment:</strong> Pick module systems use powered roller conveyors as trunk lines connecting pick zones to shipping. <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> divert totes and cartons from trunk lines to pick stations and back. Many of these systems combine live roller and <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> sections.</p>
<p><strong>Manufacturing:</strong> Assembly line applications use powered roller conveyors to move work-in-process between stations at a controlled pace. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyor</a> configurations stop and start the conveyor at set intervals for robotic pick-and-place or manual operations.</p>
<p><strong>Food and beverage:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food-grade powered roller conveyors</a> use stainless steel frames, sealed bearings, and food-contact-rated belt materials where product touches the belt. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary designs</a> feature tool-free removal for cleaning and surfaces that prevent water pooling.</p>
<p><strong>Heavy-duty pallet handling:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> handle loaded pallets between stretch wrappers, <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet dispensers</a>, and shipping docks. Heavy-wall rollers, structural frames, and close support spacing raise the per-foot rating.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Choosing the Right Powered Roller</h2>
<p><strong>Choose BDLR when:</strong> you need medium-duty case transport, adjustable drive pressure, and the option of pneumatic zero-pressure zones or mechanical minimum-pressure accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>Choose line-shaft when:</strong> you need light to medium-duty transport, simple diverting (pop-up transfers work well with line-shaft), and light passive accumulation. Line-shaft fits layouts where products divert to side spurs using pop-up <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer units</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Choose CDLR when:</strong> loads exceed the per-foot rating of belt-driven or line-shaft models, you need positive drive with no slip (such as <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyors</a>), or products have bottoms that slip on friction-driven rollers.</p>
<p><strong>Choose MDR instead when:</strong> you need built-in zero-pressure accumulation without compressed air, idle zones should stop, and product weight is within the motor roller and zone rating. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> cost more upfront but remove pneumatic infrastructure.</p>
<p>Many <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a> combine multiple powered roller types. A typical system might use BDLR for transport trunk lines, MDR for accumulation zones, CDLR for pallet handling, and <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> for take-away sections.</p>
