<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is the difference between belt-driven and line-shaft?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR uses a belt under the rollers, held in contact by pressure rollers, and friction turns the rollers. Line-shaft uses a rotating shaft with spools and an O-ring band to each roller. BDLR drive force can be set by adjusting pressure-roller contact. Line-shaft O-rings slip when product is held, and the design works well with pop-up transfers. See the full comparison in our <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyor guide</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Can powered roller conveyors accumulate product?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Continuous-run powered rollers do not accumulate without backpressure, but all three drive types come in accumulating versions. Hytrol's belt-driven zero-pressure model uses a pressure frame and pneumatic brake per zone, its line-shaft model uses air bags and brake pads, and its chain-driven models use a gearmotor per zone. Belt-driven minimum-pressure conveyors limit drive pressure so products stop with light contact. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> provide <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure accumulation</a> without compressed air.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What speed range do powered rollers support?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Speed depends on the model, drive gearing, and roller diameter, so there is no single range per drive type. Constant-speed conveyors are geared to one speed, and a variable frequency drive (VFD) allows adjustment. Specify the speed your line needs and confirm the quoted model's rated speed at your load on the data sheet.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What maintenance do powered roller conveyors require?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR needs periodic belt tension, tracking, and pressure adjustment, and belt replacement. Line-shaft needs O-ring replacement and a shaft kept free of oil. CDLR needs chain lubrication: Hytrol's manuals call for every 2,080 hours or 6 months, whichever comes first, in normal environments, and more often in harsh service. All types need motor and reducer service per the manufacturer schedule. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> have no drive belt, line shaft, or chain.</p>

</div>
</div>
